From f1a98c14839e19511a092ba25df4e7873d5f3c16 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 05:36:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] services: Samba and SSH say what they need where a person
 meets them

Hades keeps Samba and SSH safe by default, and that trips people up: a
user who turned Samba on could read its user and password only in two
lines of its settings, without the address and without what Windows and
macOS ask for; turning its password off was one switch, with no word of
what it opens; and SFTP as root failed with "No more authentication
methods available", with no reason given where the refusal is met.

Samba: a button "How to connect" (shown while Samba is on) says, at any
time, the address (the name, and each address of this system with its
numbers), the user and the password, or that there is none, and what
Windows (Remember my credentials; a login that Windows remembered for
this system) and macOS (Connect to Server, Registered User) need. It is
the same text that whoever turns Samba on is shown, now in a window to
read at one's pace. Turning "Use Samba Password Authentication" off asks
first, with "no" preselected, and says what it opens: the files of every
share, the settings of Kodi and of its add-ons with their accounts, the
folder of start-up scripts that run as root; and that Windows 11 24H2
refuses such a share by default. Anything but "yes" keeps the password.

SSH: the button "SSH Password" says whether root still has the default
password, which keeps SSH to keys, and its help says why an SFTP program
says "No more authentication methods available" and that setting a
password here is the one step that lets root log in with one. After a
new password the button says so.

Tested on the host (tools/regress/boot, stage servicessettings) with
stand-ins for Kodi. Not run in Kodi.
